    "title": "Setting Up a Hypothetical Prompt Regression Test Framework for Local LLMs",
    "body": "Developing a robust regression testing strategy for local LLMs is essential for tracking prompt efficacy. This post details a hypothetical framework for such testing. The primary goal is to establish a repeatable method for evaluating prompt changes.\n\nThe proposed test involves a curated suite of 12 fixed prompts (F1). This suite includes a mix of questions designed to elicit direct answers and others that should result in responses indicating insufficient information (F3). The testing procedure mandates running both the existing and proposed prompt versions against the identical local LLM setup, maintaining all parameters and configurations (F2). This controlled environment is key to isolating the effects of prompt modifications.\n\nThis framework is currently hypothetical; no performance data or comparative results have been generated or measured (F4). The intention is to solicit feedback on the proposed testing structure itself. We aim to discuss potential metrics and evaluation strategies that could be applied once actual tests are conducted, focusing on systematic comparison of prompt performance.",
